#!/bin/sh
# Refuse to push a commit whose TREE is structurally wrong.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS EXISTS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `main` was wiped to near-zero files TWICE in 24 hours (2026-07-31 / 2026-08-01;
# 118c636ead8 took it from 109,375 files to 4). EVERY existing guard passed both
# times. The conflict-tree guard only looks for `.jjconflict*` entries, so a tree
# truncated for any OTHER reason — a git index truncated by ENOSPC, an API
# `base_tree` landing that silently inherited an already-wiped base — sails
# straight through it. The only thing that ever caught a wipe was a human running
#
#     git ls-tree -r --name-only $C | wc -l
#
# and noticing the number. `5f1b96ad9a8` hardened the two conflict guards and
# recorded this as still open: "no automated tree-size gate — both wipes would
# have been caught by one." This is that gate.
#
# A second, subtler corruption was observed in the same window: a tree of 109,815
# files — HIGHER than the healthy 109,543 — because `src/` listed the `lib` entry
# TWICE (same mode, same sha, which git's tree format forbids) while seven other
# `src/` subdirectories were missing entirely, including all 185 files of
# `src/runtime`, leaving `src/` with 9 entries instead of 15. A floor-only size
# check is blind to that. Hence four independent checks, all fail-closed:
#
#   1. SIZE BAND  — reject a tree far too small (the wipe) AND far too large
#                   (the duplicate-entry corruption), relative to the base the
#                   push replaces, PLUS an absolute floor/ceiling so a tip that
#                   merely INHERITED an already-wiped base — delta zero, band
#                   happy — is still caught. Both wipes propagated that way.
#   2. DUPLICATES — no path may appear twice in the recursive listing. Two
#                   identical tree entries expand to two identical path lists,
#                   so this catches the observed `src/lib` duplication at any
#                   depth. `git fsck` is authoritative but takes >2 minutes on
#                   this repo, which is why it is not the gate; use it for
#                   investigation, this for gating.
#   3. src/ ENTRY BAND — `git ls-tree $C:src | wc -l` must stay in band. This is
#                   the single strongest signal available: it is one cheap call,
#                   and it fired on the real corruption (9 vs 15).
#   4. LOAD-BEARING PATHS — named directories must hold at least N files. In the
#                   observed corruption `src/runtime` held 0 against 185, which
#                   makes it a proven canary.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE NUMBERS, AND HOW TO RE-DERIVE THEM
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# All measured 2026-08-01 against origin/main 502af609d9a5. Re-derive any of them
# with the command shown beside it.
#
#   total files      109,557    git ls-tree -r --name-only $C | wc -l
#   src/ entries          15    git ls-tree --name-only $C:src | wc -l
#   src/runtime          185    git ls-tree -r --name-only $C -- src/runtime | wc -l
#   src/os             1,622    (same, -- src/os)
#   src/lib            7,456    (same, -- src/lib)
#   src/app            2,678    (same, -- src/app)
#   src/compiler       1,591    (same, -- src/compiler)
#   scripts, doc       large and stable; see CFG_PATHS floors below
#
# `src/std` holds ONE file. It is deliberately NOT a canary: a single file passes
# any non-empty test trivially and would survive nearly any corruption. Canaries
# are chosen for SIZE and STABILITY, and asserted with a MINIMUM COUNT, never
# with "> 0". Every floor below sits at roughly 60-80% of the measured value, so
# only STRUCTURAL loss trips it and ordinary deletion never does.
#
# Churn, measured across the 30 commits ending at 502af609d9a5:
#   tree size    109,539 .. 109,564
#   per commit   +1 / +2 / +4 typical; the largest single move was -25, a
#                legitimate dead-package deletion. Churn is single-digit to low
#                tens in BOTH directions.
#
# Corruption deltas, for separation:
#   wipe                109,375 ->       4     (delta -109,371)
#   duplicate-entry     109,543 -> 109,815     (delta    +272)
#
# CFG_TOL_PERMILLE = 15 per 10,000 = 0.15% => +-164 files at 109,557. That is
# ~6.5x the largest real churn (25) and ~40% BELOW the corruption delta (272), so
# the band separates both hazards from normal work with room on both sides.
#
# A lane that legitimately moves more than the band allows must STATE its
# expected post-count with `--expect-files <n>`, which recentres the band on the
# stated number and prints it in the verdict. That RECORDS the expectation in the
# command line and the log rather than bypassing the check: the absolute band,
# the duplicate check, the src/ entry band and every load-bearing floor still
# apply. There is no flag that turns a check off, and no environment variable
# overrides any threshold — if a threshold is genuinely wrong, edit it here, to
# the newly MEASURED boundary, in the same commit as the work. Do not relax it to
# make a run green.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONTRACT (matches scripts/check/check-no-conflict-tree-push.shs, 5f1b96ad9a8)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   * The verdict is ALWAYS on stdout and ALWAYS first-word-tagged PASS / FAIL /
#     ERROR, so even a `| tail` misuse shows it.
#   * "PASS — <n> commit(s) checked" with n > 0 ALWAYS. The count is the point:
#     it makes a vacuous run visible. There is no "OK".
#   * "ERROR — nothing was checked" (exit 2) whenever the guard could not do its
#     job. cwd outside a git repo is an ERROR, never a pass — a `git archive`
#     worktree under /dev/shm has no .git and is exactly where the previous
#     fail-open happened.
#   * No `git` call's exit status is discarded. That single mistake caused all
#     three fail-opens fixed in 5f1b96ad9a8.
#   * Nothing this script cares about is read through a pipe: output goes to a
#     FILE and the file is read.
#   * Everything is quoted. `for f in $paths` word-splits on filenames containing
#     spaces and caused a live miss from the correct cwd with the correct range.
#   * grep is pinned to /usr/bin/grep — the interactive default here is ugrep and
#     the two have silently disagreed.
#
# NON-VACUITY: --selftest runs automatically before every scan and is FATAL. It
# builds 14 throwaway fixtures in a temp repo — truncated tree, duplicate entry,
# missing src/runtime, shrunken src/, oversize, inherited-wipe, band-jump, and
# healthy controls — and asserts each verdict. A guard that cannot fail on its
# motivating case is worse than none.
#
# Usage:
#   check-tree-size-push.shs                       # default range main@origin..@-
#   check-tree-size-push.shs '<base>..<tip>'       # explicit range
#   check-tree-size-push.shs --expect-files 109531 '<base>..<tip>'
#   check-tree-size-push.shs --rev-list-args A --not --remotes=origin
#   check-tree-size-push.shs --selftest            # fixtures only, no scan
#
# Exit 0 = safe to push, 1 = structurally wrong tree found, 2 = could not
# determine (ERROR — nothing was checked).

set -u

SELF=check-tree-size-push

# Pin grep: the interactive default on this machine is ugrep, not GNU grep.
GREP=grep
[ -x /usr/bin/grep ] && GREP=/usr/bin/grep

# --- tuneables: see "THE NUMBERS, AND HOW TO RE-DERIVE THEM" before changing --
CFG_TOL_PERMILLE=15                 # per 10,000 => 15 = 0.15% = +-164 at 109,557
CFG_ABS_MIN=90000                   # ~18% below the measured 109,557
CFG_ABS_MAX=150000
CFG_SRC_MIN=13                      # measured 15; the corruption showed 9
CFG_SRC_MAX=25
# "<path>:<min files>" — floors at ~60-80% of measured, see table above.
CFG_PATHS='src/runtime:150 src/os:1200 src/lib:5000 src/compiler:1200 src/app:2000 scripts:500 doc:1000'

TMPROOT=""
cleanup() { [ -n "$TMPROOT" ] && rm -rf "$TMPROOT"; }
trap cleanup EXIT INT TERM

die() {
    code=$1
    shift
    echo "$SELF: ERROR — $*" >&2
    echo "$SELF: ERROR — nothing was checked (exit $code)"
    exit "$code"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# inspect_commit <repo> <commit>
#   Sets IC_FILES, IC_DUPS, IC_DUPLIST. Returns 1 and sets IC_ERR if any git call
#   failed — a git failure is never a clean result.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
inspect_commit() {
    ic_repo=$1
    ic_commit=$2
    IC_ERR=""
    IC_FILES=0
    IC_DUPS=0
    IC_DUPLIST=""

    ic_list="$TMPROOT/ls.out"
    git -C "$ic_repo" ls-tree -r --name-only "$ic_commit" >"$ic_list" 2>"$TMPROOT/ls.err"
    ic_st=$?
    if [ "$ic_st" -ne 0 ]; then
        IC_ERR="git ls-tree -r failed (status $ic_st) on $ic_commit"
        return 1
    fi

    IC_FILES=$(wc -l <"$ic_list")
    IC_FILES=$(printf '%s' "$IC_FILES" | tr -d ' ')

    # Duplicate paths. Two identical tree entries (same name, same sha) expand to
    # the same path list twice, so a recursive listing exposes them at any depth.
    # PROVED against a `git mktree` fixture with `lib` listed twice.
    sort "$ic_list" >"$TMPROOT/sorted.out" || { IC_ERR="sort failed on $ic_commit"; return 1; }
    uniq -d "$TMPROOT/sorted.out" >"$TMPROOT/dup.out" || { IC_ERR="uniq failed on $ic_commit"; return 1; }
    IC_DUPS=$(wc -l <"$TMPROOT/dup.out")
    IC_DUPS=$(printf '%s' "$IC_DUPS" | tr -d ' ')
    IC_DUPLIST=$(head -n 5 "$TMPROOT/dup.out")
    return 0
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# path_count <repo> <commit> <path>  -> echoes file count, returns git's status
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
path_count() {
    pc_out="$TMPROOT/pc.out"
    git -C "$1" ls-tree -r --name-only "$2" -- "$3" >"$pc_out" 2>/dev/null
    pc_st=$?
    [ "$pc_st" -ne 0 ] && return "$pc_st"
    pc_n=$(wc -l <"$pc_out")
    printf '%s' "$pc_n" | tr -d ' '
    return 0
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# src_entry_count <repo> <commit>
#   Echoes the number of entries directly under src/. Echoes -1 when src/ is
#   absent from the tree (a violation, not an error — a wiped tree has no src).
#   Returns non-zero only when git failed for some OTHER reason.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
src_entry_count() {
    se_out="$TMPROOT/se.out"
    git -C "$1" ls-tree --name-only "$2" -- src >"$se_out" 2>/dev/null
    se_st=$?
    [ "$se_st" -ne 0 ] && return "$se_st"
    if [ ! -s "$se_out" ]; then
        printf '%s' "-1"
        return 0
    fi
    git -C "$1" ls-tree --name-only "$2:src" >"$se_out" 2>/dev/null
    se_st=$?
    [ "$se_st" -ne 0 ] && return "$se_st"
    se_n=$(wc -l <"$se_out")
    printf '%s' "$se_n" | tr -d ' '
    return 0
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# evaluate_commit <repo> <commit> <base_files or -1>
#   Applies all four checks, printing one "  - REASON" line per violation to
#   stderr. Returns 0 clean, 1 violated, 2 could-not-determine (IC_ERR set).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
evaluate_commit() {
    ev_repo=$1
    ev_commit=$2
    ev_base=$3
    ev_bad=0

    inspect_commit "$ev_repo" "$ev_commit" || return 2
    EV_FILES=$IC_FILES

    # --- check 1a: absolute band (catches a tip that INHERITED a wiped base) --
    if [ "$IC_FILES" -lt "$CFG_ABS_MIN" ]; then
        echo "  - TREE TOO SMALL: $IC_FILES file(s); absolute floor is $CFG_ABS_MIN" >&2
        ev_bad=1
    fi
    if [ "$IC_FILES" -gt "$CFG_ABS_MAX" ]; then
        echo "  - TREE TOO LARGE: $IC_FILES file(s); absolute ceiling is $CFG_ABS_MAX" >&2
        ev_bad=1
    fi

    # --- check 1b: relative band against the base this push replaces ---------
    if [ "$ev_base" -ge 0 ]; then
        ev_tol=$(( ev_base * CFG_TOL_PERMILLE / 10000 ))
        [ "$ev_tol" -lt 1 ] && ev_tol=1
        ev_delta=$(( IC_FILES - ev_base ))
        ev_abs=$ev_delta
        [ "$ev_abs" -lt 0 ] && ev_abs=$(( 0 - ev_abs ))
        if [ "$ev_abs" -gt "$ev_tol" ]; then
            echo "  - TREE SIZE OUT OF BAND: $IC_FILES file(s) vs base $ev_base (delta $ev_delta, tolerance +-$ev_tol)" >&2
            ev_bad=1
        fi
    fi

    # --- check 2: duplicate tree entries -------------------------------------
    if [ "$IC_DUPS" -ne 0 ]; then
        echo "  - DUPLICATE TREE ENTRIES: $IC_DUPS duplicated path(s), e.g.:" >&2
        printf '%s\n' "$IC_DUPLIST" >"$TMPROOT/duphead.out"
        while IFS= read -r ev_d; do
            [ -n "$ev_d" ] && echo "      $ev_d" >&2
        done <"$TMPROOT/duphead.out"
        ev_bad=1
    fi

    # --- check 3: src/ entry band --------------------------------------------
    ev_srcn=$(src_entry_count "$ev_repo" "$ev_commit")
    ev_st=$?
    if [ "$ev_st" -ne 0 ]; then
        IC_ERR="git ls-tree failed (status $ev_st) reading src/ of $ev_commit"
        return 2
    fi
    if [ "$ev_srcn" -lt 0 ]; then
        echo "  - src/ IS ABSENT from the tree entirely" >&2
        ev_bad=1
    elif [ "$ev_srcn" -lt "$CFG_SRC_MIN" ] || [ "$ev_srcn" -gt "$CFG_SRC_MAX" ]; then
        echo "  - src/ ENTRY COUNT OUT OF BAND: $ev_srcn entries, band is $CFG_SRC_MIN..$CFG_SRC_MAX" >&2
        ev_bad=1
    fi

    # --- check 4: load-bearing paths -----------------------------------------
    # CFG_PATHS is a literal, space-separated, colon-joined list defined in this
    # file, so splitting it on a pinned IFS is safe. Every value derived FROM it
    # is quoted at every use.
    ev_old_ifs=$IFS
    IFS=' '
    for ev_spec in $CFG_PATHS; do
        IFS=$ev_old_ifs
        if [ -z "$ev_spec" ]; then IFS=' '; continue; fi
        ev_path=${ev_spec%%:*}
        ev_min=${ev_spec##*:}
        ev_n=$(path_count "$ev_repo" "$ev_commit" "$ev_path")
        ev_st=$?
        if [ "$ev_st" -ne 0 ]; then
            IFS=$ev_old_ifs
            IC_ERR="git ls-tree failed (status $ev_st) on $ev_commit -- $ev_path"
            return 2
        fi
        if [ "$ev_n" -lt "$ev_min" ]; then
            echo "  - LOAD-BEARING PATH EMPTY/SHORT: '$ev_path' has $ev_n file(s), needs >= $ev_min" >&2
            ev_bad=1
        fi
        IFS=' '
    done
    IFS=$ev_old_ifs

    return "$ev_bad"
}

# ===========================================================================
# SELFTEST — runs before every scan, FATAL. 14 fixtures.
# ===========================================================================
SELFTEST_EXPECTED=14

st_mktree() {
    # st_mktree <repo> <files-per-dir> <dir>...   -> echoes a commit sha
    st_repo=$1
    st_n=$2
    shift 2
    st_idx="$TMPROOT/st.index"
    rm -f "$st_idx"
    st_blob=$(printf 'x\n' | git -C "$st_repo" hash-object -w --stdin) || return 1
    for st_d in "$@"; do
        st_i=0
        while [ "$st_i" -lt "$st_n" ]; do
            GIT_INDEX_FILE="$st_idx" git -C "$st_repo" update-index --add \
                --cacheinfo "100644,$st_blob,$st_d/f$st_i.txt" >/dev/null || return 1
            st_i=$(( st_i + 1 ))
        done
    done
    st_tree=$(GIT_INDEX_FILE="$st_idx" git -C "$st_repo" write-tree) || return 1
    git -C "$st_repo" commit-tree "$st_tree" -m fixture </dev/null
}

selftest() {
    st_dir="$TMPROOT/selftest"
    mkdir -p "$st_dir" || return 1
    git init -q "$st_dir" >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 1
    git -C "$st_dir" config user.email selftest@local >/dev/null 2>&1
    git -C "$st_dir" config user.name selftest >/dev/null 2>&1

    # Fixture thresholds: small, so fixtures stay cheap and each one isolates the
    # check it is meant to prove. PRODUCTION thresholds are exercised by the real
    # scan that follows.
    st_s_min=$CFG_ABS_MIN;  st_s_max=$CFG_ABS_MAX
    st_s_tol=$CFG_TOL_PERMILLE; st_s_paths=$CFG_PATHS
    st_s_smin=$CFG_SRC_MIN; st_s_smax=$CFG_SRC_MAX
    CFG_ABS_MIN=100
    CFG_ABS_MAX=400
    CFG_TOL_PERMILLE=150            # 1.5%: tolerance 3 against a base of 200
    CFG_SRC_MIN=4
    CFG_SRC_MAX=6
    CFG_PATHS='src/runtime:5 src/os:5'

    st_ran=0
    st_fail=0

    st_assert() {
        # st_assert <name> <commit> <base|-1> <want 0=pass 1=fail>
        st_name=$1; st_commit=$2; st_base=$3; st_want=$4
        evaluate_commit "$st_dir" "$st_commit" "$st_base" 2>"$TMPROOT/st.err"
        st_got=$?
        st_ran=$(( st_ran + 1 ))
        if [ "$st_got" -ne "$st_want" ]; then
            echo "SELFTEST FAIL: fixture '$st_name' expected verdict $st_want, got $st_got" >&2
            cat "$TMPROOT/st.err" >&2
            st_fail=1
        fi
    }

    # healthy: 200 files, src/ has 4 entries, runtime and os hold 40 each
    st_healthy=$(st_mktree "$st_dir" 40 src/runtime src/os src/lib src/std doc) || return 1
    # truncated: the wipe case — 4 files total
    st_trunc=$(st_mktree "$st_dir" 1 src/runtime src/os src/lib src/std) || return 1
    # missing src/runtime: right size, right src/ entry count, canary gone
    st_norun=$(st_mktree "$st_dir" 50 src/os src/lib src/std src/hardware) || return 1
    # shrunken src/: right size, canaries intact, only 2 entries under src/
    st_srcsm=$(st_mktree "$st_dir" 100 src/runtime src/os) || return 1
    # small-but-shapely: 20 files, yet EVERY other check is satisfied — src/ has
    # 4 entries, both canaries clear their floors, no duplicates. Only the
    # ABSOLUTE floor can catch this, which is the shape of an inherited wipe:
    # base and tip agree, so the relative band is happy too. Added 2026-08-01
    # after a sabotage run proved no other fixture isolated the absolute floor —
    # neutering it left the selftest GREEN.
    st_small=$(st_mktree "$st_dir" 5 src/runtime src/os src/lib src/std) || return 1
    # oversize: 500 files, above the absolute ceiling
    st_over=$(st_mktree "$st_dir" 100 src/runtime src/os src/lib src/std doc) || return 1

    # duplicate-entry fixture: `src/lib` listed TWICE, mirroring the observed
    # corruption. `git mktree` writes the entries verbatim, duplicates included —
    # git's own plumbing will not otherwise produce an invalid tree.
    st_lib=$(git -C "$st_dir" rev-parse "$st_healthy^{tree}:src/lib")     || return 1
    st_run=$(git -C "$st_dir" rev-parse "$st_healthy^{tree}:src/runtime") || return 1
    st_osd=$(git -C "$st_dir" rev-parse "$st_healthy^{tree}:src/os")      || return 1
    st_std=$(git -C "$st_dir" rev-parse "$st_healthy^{tree}:src/std")     || return 1
    {
        printf '040000 tree %s\tlib\n'     "$st_lib"
        printf '040000 tree %s\tlib\n'     "$st_lib"
        printf '040000 tree %s\tos\n'      "$st_osd"
        printf '040000 tree %s\truntime\n' "$st_run"
        printf '040000 tree %s\tstd\n'     "$st_std"
    } >"$TMPROOT/mktree.src"
    st_srctree=$(git -C "$st_dir" mktree <"$TMPROOT/mktree.src") || return 1
    printf '040000 tree %s\tsrc\n' "$st_srctree" >"$TMPROOT/mktree.top"
    st_toptree=$(git -C "$st_dir" mktree <"$TMPROOT/mktree.top") || return 1
    st_dupc=$(git -C "$st_dir" commit-tree "$st_toptree" -m dupfixture </dev/null) || return 1

    # --- 11 fixtures that MUST FAIL ---------------------------------------
    st_assert truncated-tree      "$st_trunc"   -1  1   # the wipe, no base
    st_assert truncated-vs-base   "$st_trunc"   200 1   # the wipe, healthy base
    st_assert inherited-wipe      "$st_trunc"   4   1   # base ALSO wiped: delta 0
    st_assert absolute-floor-only "$st_small"   20  1   # ONLY the abs floor fires
    st_assert missing-src-runtime "$st_norun"   -1  1   # proven canary at 0
    st_assert src-entries-shrunk  "$st_srcsm"   -1  1   # src/ 2 entries, band 4..6
    st_assert duplicate-entry     "$st_dupc"    -1  1   # src/lib listed twice
    st_assert duplicate-vs-base   "$st_dupc"    200 1   # ... independent of base
    st_assert oversize-absolute   "$st_over"    -1  1   # 500 > ceiling 400
    st_assert oversize-vs-base    "$st_over"    200 1
    st_assert band-jump           "$st_healthy" 160 1   # +40 vs tolerance 2

    # --- 3 fixtures that MUST PASS ----------------------------------------
    st_assert healthy-no-base     "$st_healthy" -1  0
    st_assert healthy-exact-base  "$st_healthy" 200 0
    st_assert healthy-small-churn "$st_healthy" 198 0   # delta 2 == tolerance 2

    CFG_ABS_MIN=$st_s_min;  CFG_ABS_MAX=$st_s_max
    CFG_TOL_PERMILLE=$st_s_tol; CFG_PATHS=$st_s_paths
    CFG_SRC_MIN=$st_s_smin; CFG_SRC_MAX=$st_s_smax

    if [ "$st_ran" -ne "$SELFTEST_EXPECTED" ]; then
        echo "SELFTEST FAIL: expected $SELFTEST_EXPECTED fixtures, ran $st_ran -- the harness did not run" >&2
        return 1
    fi
    [ "$st_fail" -eq 0 ] || return 1
    echo "$SELF: selftest $st_ran/$SELFTEST_EXPECTED fixtures correct (11 must-fail, 3 must-pass)"
    return 0
}

# ===========================================================================
# MAIN
# ===========================================================================
TMPROOT=$(mktemp -d) || { echo "$SELF: ERROR — cannot mktemp" >&2
                          echo "$SELF: ERROR — nothing was checked (exit 2)"; exit 2; }

if ! selftest; then
    echo "$SELF: ERROR — selftest failed; the detector is not trustworthy" >&2
    echo "$SELF: ERROR — nothing was checked (exit 2)"
    exit 2
fi

if [ "${1:-}" = "--selftest" ]; then
    echo "$SELF: PASS — $SELFTEST_EXPECTED fixture(s) checked (selftest only, scan skipped)"
    exit 0
fi

expect_files=-1
if [ "${1:-}" = "--expect-files" ]; then
    shift
    [ "$#" -gt 0 ] || die 2 "--expect-files needs a number"
    expect_files=$1
    case "$expect_files" in
        ''|*[!0-9]*) die 2 "--expect-files '$expect_files' is not a non-negative integer" ;;
    esac
    shift
fi

range="${1:-}"
explicit=0
rawmode=0
[ -n "$range" ] && explicit=1
case "$range" in
    --rev-list-args)
        rawmode=1
        shift
        [ "$#" -gt 0 ] || die 2 "--rev-list-args needs arguments"
        ;;
    --*)
        die 2 "unknown option '$range'; raw mode is '--rev-list-args <args>...'"
        ;;
esac

# --- 1. cwd must be inside a git repository ---------------------------------
toplevel=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null) || toplevel=""
if [ -z "$toplevel" ]; then
    die 2 "cwd '$(pwd)' is not inside a git repository.
  This guard must run from the repo root of the clone you are about to push.
  A 'git archive' worktree under /dev/shm or /run/user/1000 has no .git and
  CANNOT be checked from — cd to the real clone and re-run."
fi
toplevel=$(CDPATH= cd -- "$toplevel" && pwd -P) || die 2 "cannot canonicalise toplevel"

# --- 2. cross-check: are we checking the repo this script ships in? ---------
script_dir=$(CDPATH= cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")" 2>/dev/null && pwd -P) || script_dir=""
script_root=""
if [ -n "$script_dir" ]; then
    script_root=$(CDPATH= cd -- "$script_dir/../.." 2>/dev/null && pwd -P) || script_root=""
fi
if [ -n "$script_root" ] && [ "$script_root" != "$toplevel" ]; then
    if [ -e "$script_root/.git" ]; then
        die 2 "repository mismatch.
  This script lives in the git repo at: $script_root
  but cwd resolves to the git repo at:  $toplevel
  Refusing to report on a different repository than the one being guarded."
    fi
    echo "$SELF: WARNING — script at $script_dir is outside the repo being checked ($toplevel)" >&2
fi

# --- 3. resolve the range, fail closed --------------------------------------
base_rev=""
if [ "$explicit" -eq 0 ]; then
    base=$(jj --ignore-working-copy log -r 'main@origin' --no-graph -T 'commit_id' 2>/dev/null) || base=""
    tip=$(jj --ignore-working-copy log -r '@-' --no-graph -T 'commit_id' 2>/dev/null) || tip=""
    if [ -z "$base" ] || [ -z "$tip" ]; then
        die 2 "cannot resolve main@origin..@- via jj (is jj available and is this a jj workspace?).
  Pass an explicit range instead, e.g. $SELF '<base-sha>..<tip-sha>'."
    fi
    range="$base..$tip"
    base_rev="$base"
elif [ "$rawmode" -eq 0 ]; then
    case "$range" in
        *..*) : ;;
        *) die 2 "explicit range '$range' does not contain '..'.
  A bare revision means 'all history reachable from it' to git, which is not
  what this guard is for. Pass '<base>..<tip>'." ;;
    esac
fi

# --- 4. both endpoints must exist as commits in THIS repo -------------------
if [ "$rawmode" -eq 0 ]; then
    left=${range%%..*}
    right=${range#*..}
    right=${right#.}          # tolerate the A...B form
    [ -n "$left" ]  || die 2 "range '$range' has an empty left endpoint"
    [ -n "$right" ] || die 2 "range '$range' has an empty right endpoint"
    for ep in "$left" "$right"; do
        git rev-parse --verify --quiet "$ep^{commit}" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
            || die 2 "revision '$ep' (from range '$range') does not exist in the repository at $toplevel.
  Either you are in the wrong clone, or the commit was never fetched here."
    done
    base_rev="$left"
fi

# --- 5. establish the reference count for the relative band -----------------
# Normally the base is the range's left endpoint: exactly the tree the push
# replaces. `--expect-files` overrides it with a STATED number, which is printed
# in the verdict so the expectation is on the record. In raw rev-list mode (a
# brand-new ref) there is no base; the relative band is then unavailable and the
# verdict SAYS SO. The absolute band still applies, and that is the check which
# catches a wipe, so this is a narrowing rather than a fail-open.
base_files=-1
base_source=""
if [ "$expect_files" -ge 0 ]; then
    base_files=$expect_files
    base_source="stated via --expect-files"
elif [ -n "$base_rev" ]; then
    if inspect_commit "$toplevel" "$base_rev"; then
        base_files=$IC_FILES
        base_source="measured at base $base_rev"
    else
        die 2 "cannot measure the base commit '$base_rev': $IC_ERR"
    fi
fi

# --- 6. enumerate the range, checking git's exit status ---------------------
if [ "$rawmode" -eq 1 ]; then
    revs=$(git rev-list "$@" 2>/dev/null)
    rl=$?
    range="rev-list-args: $*"
else
    revs=$(git rev-list "$range" 2>/dev/null)
    rl=$?
fi
[ "$rl" -eq 0 ] || die 2 "git rev-list '$range' failed with status $rl"

n=0
for rev in $revs; do n=$((n + 1)); done

if [ "$n" -eq 0 ]; then
    if [ "$explicit" -eq 1 ]; then
        die 2 "explicit range '$range' resolved to 0 commits.
  You asked this guard to check a range and there is nothing in it. That is a
  caller error (wrong base, wrong tip, or endpoints already equal) — it is NOT
  a clean result, so it is reported as a failure rather than a pass."
    fi
    echo "$SELF: NOTHING TO PUSH — $range is empty; NO COMMITS WERE CHECKED"
    exit 0
fi

# --- 7. the actual check ----------------------------------------------------
found=0
checked=0
for rev in $revs; do
    evaluate_commit "$toplevel" "$rev" "$base_files"
    st=$?
    if [ "$st" -eq 2 ]; then
        die 2 "cannot verify commit $rev: ${IC_ERR:-unknown git failure}"
    fi
    checked=$(( checked + 1 ))
    if [ "$st" -ne 0 ]; then
        echo "STRUCTURALLY WRONG TREE: $rev $(git log -1 --format=%s "$rev" 2>/dev/null)" >&2
        found=$(( found + 1 ))
    fi
done

if [ "$checked" -eq 0 ]; then
    die 2 "the scan loop examined 0 commits although the range held $n — the harness did not run"
fi

if [ "$found" -ne 0 ]; then
    cat >&2 <<'EOF'

DO NOT PUSH. The commits above have structurally wrong trees. `main` has been
wiped to near-zero files twice this way, each time with every other guard green.

Investigate before doing anything else:
  git ls-tree -r --name-only <sha> | wc -l      # the number a human spots
  git ls-tree <sha>:src                         # duplicate / missing entries
  git fsck --no-dangling <sha>                  # authoritative, but >2min here

If the tree is genuinely correct and merely large, either re-run stating the
expected count (--expect-files <n>, which is recorded in the verdict), or raise
the threshold in this script TO THE MEASURED BOUNDARY in the same commit. Do not
relax it to make a run green.
EOF
    echo "$SELF: FAIL — $checked commit(s) checked in $range, $found structurally wrong"
    exit 1
fi

if [ "$base_files" -ge 0 ]; then
    echo "$SELF: PASS — $checked commit(s) checked in $range, reference $base_files file(s) ($base_source), 0 structural faults (repo $toplevel)"
else
    echo "$SELF: PASS — $checked commit(s) checked in $range, no reference count available so absolute band only, 0 structural faults (repo $toplevel)"
fi
exit 0
